---
import { UNKNOWN_STATUSES } from '@ecfr-atlas/core';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { STRINGS } from '../constants/strings';
import { statusExplanation, statusLabel } from '../data/format';
import * as route from '../data/routes';
/**
 * Methodology.
 *
 * This page is the credibility mechanism. A derived measurement that does not publish its
 * definition is a number someone has to take on trust, and the predecessor spent that trust.
 * Everything here is stated precisely enough to be argued with.
 *
 * The rules themselves stay inline as prose: nearly every sentence carries a `<code>` sample or
 * an `<em>` inside it, and this page's whole job is to be read end to end. Its headings and the
 * callout title are in STRINGS.
 *
 * The unknown-status list is core's `UNKNOWN_STATUSES`, not a local copy. This page is the
 * public explanation of what those statuses mean, so a status added to core and missing here
 * would leave the site explaining three of four reasons and never say so.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title={STRINGS.methodology.title}
  description={STRINGS.methodology.description}
  section="about"
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow={STRINGS.methodology.eyebrow}
    title={STRINGS.methodology.title}
    standfirst={STRINGS.methodology.standfirst}
  />

  <div class="prose-page">
    <h2 id="what-is-a-word">{STRINGS.methodology.headings.whatIsAWord}</h2>
    <p>
      After the excluded elements below are removed, the remaining text is split into tokens and
      each token is counted as one word
      <strong>if and only if it contains at least one Unicode letter or number</strong>. The
      alphanumeric test is <code>/[\p&#123;L&#125;\p&#123;N&#125;]/u</code>. There is no Unicode
      normalisation pass; the text is scanned code point by code point exactly as eCFR publishes
      it. Three kinds of character end a token:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>any Unicode whitespace character;</li>
      <li>
        en dash, em dash and horizontal bar (<code>–</code>, <code>—</code>, <code>―</code>),
        because they join independent words in prose;
      </li>
      <li>
        a run of <strong>two or more</strong> hyphen-minus characters. A single hyphen does
        <em>not</em> split.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Zero-width and invisible formatting characters (U+00AD soft hyphen, U+200B–U+200D, U+2060,
      U+FEFF) are deleted rather than treated as boundaries, so a word broken across a printed line
      counts once.
    </p>
    <p>Consequences of that rule, spelled out because they are where reasonable people differ:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>60.1</code> is one word. Section numbers count.</li>
      <li><code>cross-reference</code> is one word, not two. A single hyphen joins.</li>
      <li><code>and/or</code> is one word. Slashes, apostrophes and periods do not split.</li>
      <li>
        <code>§</code> and <code>—</code> on their own are not words: no letter, no digit. So
        <code>§ 60.1</code> is one word, not two.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Paragraph designators are counted.</strong> A bare <code>(a)</code> contains the
        letter “a”, so it is one word, and <code>(a)(2)(iii)</code> is one word too. They are part
        of the regulation and they are how it is cited.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>words—including</code> is two words. An em dash separates even with no space around
        it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Table contents are not counted at all</strong> — see the exclusions below. A table
        of emission limits is regulation, and the counts on this site do not reflect it. This is
        the single largest known limitation of the measurement.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="exclusions">{STRINGS.methodology.headings.exclusions}</h2>
    <p>
      Counting a part's raw XML would count its scaffolding. The following eCFR elements are
      removed before tokenisation, along with their contents:
    </p>
    <dl>
      <dt><code>HEAD</code></dt>
      <dd>Headings. They repeat the citation already counted in the structure.</dd>
      <dt><code>AUTH</code>, <code>SECAUTH</code></dt>
      <dd>
        The statutory authority note, at part level and at section level. It cites the enabling
        statute; it is not the regulation text. It is shown on every part page, just not counted.
      </dd>
      <dt><code>SOURCE</code></dt>
      <dd>Federal Register source notes — publication provenance, not regulatory content.</dd>
      <dt><code>CITA</code></dt>
      <dd>Citation notes.</dd>
      <dt><code>CONTENTS</code></dt>
      <dd>Generated tables of contents, which would double-count every section heading.</dd>
      <dt><code>EAR</code></dt>
      <dd>The running-head “ear” printed in the margin of the paper CFR.</dd>
      <dt><code>FTNT</code></dt>
      <dd>Footnote apparatus.</dd>
      <dt><code>XREF</code></dt>
      <dd>
        eCFR's own editorial navigation — “Link to an amendment published at 91 FR 43564” — which
        appears and disappears as amendments pend. Counting it would make a section's total move
        for a reason that has nothing to do with the regulation. Measured at 17 occurrences and
        204 words in 40 CFR 60, and absent from 1 CFR entirely.
      </dd>
      <dt>
        Table markup: <code>TABLE</code>, <code>CAPTION</code>, <code>THEAD</code>,
        <code>TBODY</code>, <code>TFOOT</code>, <code>TR</code>, <code>TH</code>, <code>TD</code>,
        and the GPO vocabulary <code>GPOTABLE</code>, <code>BOXHD</code>, <code>CHED</code>,
        <code>ROW</code>, <code>ENT</code>, <code>TTITLE</code>, <code>TDESC</code>,
        <code>TNOTE</code>
      </dt>
      <dd>
        Removed <strong>with their contents</strong>, so no text inside a table is counted —
        neither the column labels nor the values. The reasoning is that a table's “words” are
        largely repeated headers and units, and counting them makes a title's total a function of
        how its tables were laid out rather than of how much regulation it contains. It is a
        defensible line and it is also a real limitation: a part whose substance lives in tables
        measures low here, and this site does not currently publish a separate table-cell count.
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Two things that look like apparatus are deliberately <em>kept</em>, having been checked.
      <code>HD1</code>/<code>HD2</code>/<code>HD3</code> — undesignated centre headings inside a
      section body — are counted, because unlike <code>HEAD</code> they appear in no ancestor's
      table of contents and so duplicate nothing: 1,997 of them and 9,418 words in 40 CFR 60.
      <code>MATH</code> is kept too, but eCFR publishes equations as raster images, so it
      contributes nothing. Equations are effectively invisible to this measurement.
    </p>
    <p>
      The exclusions matter more than they sound. Measured against 1 CFR Chapter I, the heading and
      note boilerplate is <strong>18.4% of the text</strong>. A count that includes it is not
      slightly high; it is high by nearly a fifth, and unevenly, because boilerplate density varies
      with how finely a part is subdivided.
    </p>

    <h2 id="parsing">{STRINGS.methodology.headings.parsing}</h2>
    <p>
      With a real XML parser, never a regular expression. That is a hard rule in this codebase and
      it is not stylistic: the CFR's XML does not have the regular structure a pattern would need.
      Nesting depth is not sequential — title 7 jumps from <code>DIV2</code> straight to
      <code>DIV5</code> in 35 places — and title 40 contains 19,134 untyped <code>DIV</code>
      elements sitting inside sections. Any pattern that appears to work does so by accident on
      the titles it was tested against.
    </p>
    <p>
      A second trap sits at the fetch layer. eCFR's <code>?chapter=</code> and
      <code>?subtitle=</code> query parameters validate but do not slice: they return the entire
      title. Only <code>?part=</code> and <code>?section=</code> narrow the response. Code that
      assumes otherwise silently measures a whole title and attributes it to one chapter, which is
      exactly the failure that produced a 12.7&times; over-credit in this project's predecessor.
    </p>

    <h2 id="scopes">{STRINGS.methodology.headings.scopes}</h2>
    <p>
      eCFR gives each agency a list of CFR references. A reference may name a title, a subtitle, a
      chapter, a subchapter or a part, and the fields nest — one reference can carry several at
      once. <strong>The narrowest level present defines the scope.</strong> A reference naming
      both Chapter I and Part 60 is a reference to Part 60, and crediting the agency with the
      whole chapter would over-count it by the size of everything else in that chapter.
    </p>
    <p>
      Twelve of the 487 references in the corpus carry a chapter alongside something narrower.
      They are the reason this rule is stated first and enforced in a shared function rather than
      re-implemented per caller.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sharing">{STRINGS.methodology.headings.sharing}</h2>
    <p>Two totals are published for every agency, and they answer different questions.</p>
    <dl>
      <dt>Attributed</dt>
      <dd>
        Every scope the agency claims, counted in full. Answers “how much regulation is this
        agency responsible for?”. It is correct per agency and wrong in aggregate — summing it
        across agencies counts each shared scope once per claimant.
      </dd>
      <dt>Deduplicated</dt>
      <dd>
        A scope claimed by <em>n</em> agencies contributes its words divided by <em>n</em> to each
        of them. Answers “how does the CFR divide up?”, and sums correctly to the corpus. This is
        the dashboard headline.
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Even division is a <strong>modelling choice, not a measurement</strong>. Nothing in the data
      says how the administrative burden of a jointly held scope actually splits, so the site
      applies the only weighting that requires no invention and says so wherever the number
      appears. Rounding to whole words leaves a residual of at most one word per claimant.
      The full list of overlaps is on <a href={route.sharedJurisdiction()}>shared jurisdiction</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The dashboard also publishes a third figure, “words under shared jurisdiction”, and it is
      worth being exact about how the three relate, because it is easy to state wrongly. Write
      <em>w</em> for a shared scope's word count and <em>n</em> for its number of claimants. The
      shared figure is the sum of <em>w</em> — each shared scope counted once, so it is directly
      comparable with the deduplicated corpus total. The gap between the attributed and
      deduplicated totals is the sum of (<em>n</em> − 1) × <em>w</em>, which is larger: a scope
      claimed by six agencies contributes five extra copies of itself, not one. The two are equal
      only where every shared scope has exactly two claimants, and in this corpus they have
      between two and six.
    </p>

    <h2 id="rollups">{STRINGS.methodology.headings.rollups}</h2>
    <p>
      A parent's count is the sum of its children's — but only when <strong>every</strong> child is
      known. If one descendant could not be measured, the parent is reported as unmeasured rather
      than as a partial sum. This is deliberate and occasionally frustrating: a partial sum
      under-reports, and an under-report looks like a plausible number rather than an error, which
      makes it worse than an obvious gap.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reserved nodes are the exception. A reserved part genuinely contains nothing, so it
      contributes a measured zero and does not spoil the roll-up.
    </p>

    <h2 id="unknown">{STRINGS.methodology.headings.unknown}</h2>
    <p>
      An em dash is never a zero and never a placeholder. It means the value was not measured, and
      hovering it gives the reason. There are four:
    </p>
    <dl>
      {
        UNKNOWN_STATUSES.map((status) => (
          <>
            <dt>
              <code>{status}</code> — {statusLabel(status)}
            </dt>
            <dd>{statusExplanation(status)}</dd>
          </>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
    <p>
      Every node in this state is listed, grouped and counted on
      <a href={route.dataQuality()}> data quality</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="dates">{STRINGS.methodology.headings.dates}</h2>
    <p>
      eCFR publishes on business days only — 57 issue dates in an 84-day window, none at a
      weekend, with a median of 48 changed sections per publishing day. Charts on this site do not
      smooth across those gaps.
    </p>
    <p>
      Amendments carry two dates and they disagree in about half of all rows. This site keys
      content on <strong>issue date</strong>, not amendment date, because 40.4% of amendment dates
      fall before eCFR's 2017-01-01 full-text horizon; fetching at an amendment date returns
      nothing, and a naive comparison then renders “section added” for a section that has existed
      for decades.
    </p>

    <Callout tone="accent" class="mt-8" title={STRINGS.methodology.oursCalloutTitle}>
      <p>
        No federal agency publishes a word count of the CFR. Every figure on this site is a
        derived measurement produced by this project's parser against the eCFR's public API. They
        are reproducible — the code and the pipeline are open — but they are not official, and
        they should be cited as this project's measurements rather than as government statistics.
        See <a href={route.about()}>about and attribution</a>.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </div>
</Base>
